## Context

ShareTown handled returned and unsellable e-commerce packages and moved them to new locations. We built the app from scratch, with views for drivers and for people managing storage and inventory. The team was two senior engineers: I owned Android and the Angular web side, and an engineer in Colombia owned iOS.

## Decision

At the time, Ionic's community plugins were limited and unstable. Rather than depend on them, we wrote our own native plugins. I owned GPS tracking on Android, and we also added barcode-scanning and camera plugins. Owning the native layer made the app noticeably more stable.

## Trade-off

Custom plugins need engineers comfortable with both Angular and native code, and those are hard to hire. We offset that with thorough documentation and a full handover to the client. My teammate stayed on and saw the app through release.
